cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Snapdragon 778G (Adreno 642L) and Snapdragon 8 Gen 2 (Adreno 740) llama.cpp CPU vs OpenCL benchmark: GPU offload is slower than CPU on both"
message: "If you use these measurements, please cite this repository."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "vovaxa55"
    name: "vovaxa55"
    website: "https://github.com/vovaxa55"
date-released: 2026-10-24
version: 1.1.0
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/vovaxa55/snapdragon778g-llama-cpp-opencl-bench"
url: "https://github.com/vovaxa55/snapdragon778g-llama-cpp-opencl-bench"
abstract: >-
  Benchmark of llama.cpp (fork 0.7.0-dev, commit 19ab5a5) on an OPPO Reno7 5G (Snapdragon 778G,
  Adreno 642L, 8 GB LPDDR4x, Android 13) running in Termux. Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct Q4_K_M reaches 28.8 tok/s
  prompt processing (256 tokens) and 7.45 tok/s generation (64 tokens) on the CPU with 4 threads
  (-ngl 0), and every GPU offload level is slower: -ngl 8 = 25.5/5.25, -ngl 14 = 22.7/4.14,
  -ngl 20 = 20.74/3.32, -ngl 37 = 17.37/2.24. Decode on the CPU already runs at ~91 % of the LPDDR4x-4266
  memory-bandwidth roofline (2.094 GB per token), so GPU offload cannot help. The Adreno OpenCL driver also
  requires source patches to run at all (vendor compiler SIGSEGV on multi-kernel programs, F16 GEMM and
  flash-attention kernels fail to build). Includes thermal-throttling measurements: 29 % lower prompt
  throughput at 45-47 C versus a cold device, ~2 % generation difference.
  Second device, same protocol (2026-10-24): OnePlus 11 (Snapdragon 8 Gen 2 / SM8550, Adreno 740 with 6
  compute units, 14 GB LPDDR5X, Android 16, llama.cpp commit 6b9ba4f) reaches 45.11 tok/s prompt and 13.81
  tok/s generation on the CPU (-ngl 0 -t 4) and 44.30/12.52 under sustained load; GPU -ngl 18 gives
  36.97/12.97 (pp -18 %, tg -6 %) and 27.84/9.41 sustained (-37 % pp / -25 % tg). No offload level beats
  -ngl 0: prompt throughput falls monotonically to 31.78 tok/s at -ngl 36 (-24 %) while generation stays
  flat. The Adreno 740 driver needs none of the 778G patches. 1 Hz thermal telemetry shows the
  throttling trigger is SoC-wide (scaling_max_freq is cut at ~50-52 C package / 85-91 C big-core for both
  backends) and that in the sustained GPU run the prime core was pinned at 864 MHz in 155 of 157 sampled
  seconds, versus 1.84-2.48 GHz on the CPU run.
keywords:
  - llama.cpp
  - OpenCL
  - Adreno
  - Snapdragon 778G
  - Snapdragon 8 Gen 2
  - Adreno 740
  - OnePlus 11
  - thermal throttling
  - Termux
  - Android
  - LLM inference
  - benchmark
  - ggml
